0.2 Core Idea
The central design rule is simple:

A Quarto Manual is an ordered set of independent executable pages.

Each page is a contract with three parts:

prereq: prove that the page is allowed to run;
procedure: do one or more pieces of work;
check: prove that the work succeeded.

This avoids hidden state between pages.
If page 3 depends on page 2, page 3 should enforce that dependency in its prerequisite block by checking visible project state:

required files exist;
required directories exist;
a previous test suite passes;
a required tool is installed;
a prior configuration file contains the expected values.

Pages therefore share a project, not a notebook kernel.

2.4 Authoring Notes
Keep page state local. Do not rely on notebook variables from another page. Instead:

use prerequisite blocks to enforce dependency on earlier pages;
use procedure blocks to create visible project artifacts;
use check blocks to test those artifacts directly.

That keeps each page independent, rerunnable, and easy to reason about.
